# Local-only agent guidance (not published; comments in tracked files must not
# reference these)
CLAUDE.md
RULES.md
task.md
PLAN.md
PLAN-calibration-lv2.md
.claude/

# A local set of captures for the offline proofs to run against. These are NOT
# this project's to publish: a capture is a recording made by whoever trained
# it, under their own terms, and the code's MIT grant does not reach it. The
# host ships none and resolves no bank by a tracked path — the four banks are
# whatever the user loaded — so nothing here depends on this directory existing.
# The proofs find it through --captures or the test-captures environment
# variable.
#
# Keep it that way: a capture committed here is a capture republished to
# everyone who clones, and deleting it later does not take it back out of the
# history.
captures/

# Rack presets and scan caches written while testing. Derived state that names
# absolute paths on whichever machine produced it.
*.rack
scancache
pluginindex

# The ASIO SDK, which must NEVER be committed here, in whole or in part.
#
# This is a licence condition and not tidiness. Steinberg's agreement forbids the licensee to
# "sell, license, give-away and/or distribute the SDK or parts of it for the use as software
# developer kit in any way, for example, integrated in any framework application, on any medium,
# including the Internet" — so a vendored copy, a checked-in header, or a stray asio.h anywhere in
# the working tree is a breach, and unlike most mistakes in this tree, pushing one cannot be taken
# back by a later deletion. (The reference implementations get this wrong: RtAudio vendors an ASIO
# snapshot in-repository and iPlug2 inherits it. It is not a precedent to follow.)
#
# The SDK reaches the Windows build through scripts/build-win-deps.sh and the untracked dependency
# sysroot, and through nothing else. These names are guarded so a stray copy cannot be staged by
# accident; the phase gate sweeps for the same names, because the ignore rule stops a copy arriving
# and the sweep catches one that arrived another way.
#
# Our own backend is asiobackend.{h,cpp}, which none of these patterns matches.
asio.h
asio.cpp
asiosys.h
iasiodrv.h
asiodrvr.h
asiodrvr.cpp
asiodrivers.h
asiodrivers.cpp
asiolist.h
asiolist.cpp
ASIOConvertSamples.h
ASIOConvertSamples.cpp

# Personal CMake configure presets (local reference-tree paths).
CMakeUserPresets.json

# Build directories
build/
build-*/
cmake-build-*/

# CMake generated
CMakeCache.txt
CMakeFiles/
cmake_install.cmake
Makefile
*.cmake
!CMakeLists.txt
# ...but cmake/ holds SOURCE scripts, not generated ones — the MinGW toolchain
# file and the resource-embed script both live there, and both are named on a
# command line rather than being generated. Without this negation the blanket
# *.cmake above keeps them out of a fresh clone, and the Windows build and the
# self-contained standalone are each unreproducible. Both parent projects had to
# fix exactly this. Generated .cmake files all live under the build directories
# already ignored above.
!cmake/*.cmake

